lement sizePositionLocked="false" border-color="#000000" type="Text" uniqueID="element12" deletable="" border-width="0" y="750" x="717" width="199" editable="true" name="Text-8" height="16" padding="0" groupID="3" bgcolor="" locked="" templateID=""><Paragraph><Segment font-style="bold">Neolithic Yayoi     2000-1000bc</Segment></Paragraph></Element><?property ignore-whitespace="true"?><?property ignore-whitespace="false"?><Element border-color="#000000" editable="true" border-width="0" y="811" name="Text-9" x="46" templateID="" width="300" locked="" bgcolor="" type="Text" padding="0" deletable="" sizePositionLocked="false" height="20" uniqueID="element13"><Paragraph><Segment>Click here to add text.</Segment></Paragraph></Element><?property ignore-whitespace="true"?><?property ignore-whitespace="false"?><Element border-color="#000000" editable="true" border-width="0" y="823" name="Text-10" x="42" templateID="" width="849" locked="" bgcolor="" type="Text" padding="0" deletable="" sizePositionLocked="false" height="20" uniqueID="element14"><Paragraph><Segment>Click here to add text.</Segment></Paragraph></Element><?property ignore-whitespace="true"?><?property ignore-whitespace="false"?><Element border-color="#000000" editable="true" border-width="0" y="818" name="Text-11" x="39" templateID="" width="916" locked="" bgcolor="" type="Text" padding="0" deletable="" sizePositionLocked="false" height="206" uniqueID="element15"><Paragraph><Segment font-size="12" font-style="bold" color="#F99976">Pottery made during the Yayoi period began displaying colored engobes and a more painterly style. These Neolithic farmers made pottery to be used for storage, yet the desire to decorate was of great importance to these ancient craftsmen.</Segment></Paragraph><Paragraph><Segment font-size="12" font-style="bold" color="#F99976"/></Paragraph><Paragraph><Segment font-size="12" font-style="bold" color="#F99976"/></Paragraph><Paragraph><Segment font-size="12" font-style="bold" color="#F99976">As we move towards the 5th and 6th centuries, we see a simple feldspathic glaze being employed on the Sue period pottery. This beautiful glossy gray-green surface was the beginnings of Japanese celedons. As this glossy, clean surfaced ware was developing, the rugged Bizen and Tamba ware were being produced into the 1300's. Thick, bubbly glazes laden with ash, unglazed feet and uneven rims exemplify the beauty and total freedom of Bizen ware.  With their irregularity and asymmetry, these sumptuous  forms exhibit the essence of the Japanese concepts of beauty.</Segment></Paragraph><Paragraph><Segment>Click here to add text.</Segment></Paragraph></Element><?property ignore-whitespace="true"?><Element isImageType="true" sizePositionLocked="false" name="Add an Image-5" link_target="_self" maintainProportion="true" originalSource="" src="Sue_5-6th_c.jpg" height="276" alt="" editable="true" resizeable="true" locked="" deletable="" groupID="4" width="188" WOHratio="0.6828703703703703" y="1054" link="" x="49" uniqueID="element16" fileType="IMAGEFILTER" templateID="" type="ImportedImage" zoom="1" offsetY="0" offsetX="0" onPublish="ImportedImage" onPreview="ImportedImage" bucketRatio="0.6828703703703703" imageRatio="0.6828703703703703" rotate="0" brightness="0" contrast="0" colorEffect="" tintRed="0" tintGreen="0" tintBlue="0" borderWidth="0" borderColor="#52A8EC" borderRadius="0" boxShadow="none" glowColor="#52A8EC" origWidth="188" origHeight="276"/><?property ignore-whitespace="false"?><Element sizePositionLocked="false" border-color="#000000" type="Text" uniqueID="element17" deletable="" border-width="0" y="1314" x="49" width="111" editable="true" name="Text-12" height="20" padding="0" groupID="4" bgcolor="" locked="" templateID=""><Paragraph><Segment font-style="bold">Sue 5th-6th C ad</Segment></Paragraph></Element><?property ignore-whitespace="true"?><Element isImageType="true" sizePositionLocked="false" name="Add an Image-6" link_target="_self" maintainProportion="true" originalSource="" src="Bizen_12-13th_c.jpg" height="271" alt="" editable="true" resizeable="true" locked="" deletable="" groupID="5" width="185" WOHratio="0.6828703703703703" y="1054" link="" x="291" uniqueID="element18" fileType="IMAGEFILTER" templateID="" type="ImportedImage" zoom="1" offsetY="0" offsetX="0" onPublish="ImportedImage" onPreview="ImportedImage" bucketRatio="0.6828703703703703" imageRatio="0.6828703703703703" rotate="0" brightness="0" contrast="0" colorEffect="" tintRed="0" tintGreen="0" tintBlue="0" borderWidth="0" borderColor="#52A8EC" borderRadius="0" boxShadow="none" glowColor="#52A8EC" origWidth="185" origHeight="271"/><?property ignore-whitespace="false"?><Element sizePositionLocked="false" border-color="#000000" type="Text" uniqueID="element19" deletable="" border-width="0" y="1310" x="292" width="196" editable="true" name="Text-13" height="20" padding="0" groupID="5" bgcolor="" locked="" templateID=""><Paragraph><Segment font-style="bold">Bizen                  1200-1300ad</Segment></Paragraph></Element><?property ignore-whitespace="true"?><Element isImageType="true" sizePositionLocked="false" name="Add an Image-7" link_target="_self" maintainProportion="true" originalSource="" src="Seto_12-13th_c.jpg" height="202" alt="" editable="true" resizeable="true" locked="" deletable="" groupID="6" width="229" WOHratio="1.1322033898305084" y="1083" link="" x="502" uniqueID="element20" fileType="IMAGEFILTER" templateID="" type="ImportedImage" zoom="1" offsetY="0" offsetX="0" onPublish="ImportedImage" onPreview="ImportedImage" bucketRatio="1.1322033898305084" imageRatio="1.1322033898305084" rotate="0" brightness="0" contrast="0" colorEffect="" tintRed="0" tintGreen="0" tintBlue="0" borderWidth="0" borderColor="#52A8EC" borderRadius="0" boxShadow="none" glowColor="#52A8EC" origWidth="229" origHeight="202"/><?property ignore-whitespace="false"?><Element sizePositionLocked="false" border-color="#000000" type="Text" uniqueID="element21" deletable="" border-width="0" y="1270" x="502" width="242" editable="true" name="Text-14" height="16" padding="0" groupID="6" bgcolor="" locked="" templateID=""><Paragraph><Segment font-style="bold">Seto                               1200-1300ad</Segment></Paragraph></Element><?property ignore-whitespace="true"?><Element isImageType="true" sizePositionLocked="false" name="Add an Image-8" link_target="_self" maintainProportion="true" originalSource="" src="Tamba_storage_jar_Kama_Kura_per.c_1200.jpg" height="274" alt="" editable="true" resizeable="true" locked="" deletable="" groupID="7" width="187" WOHratio="0.6828703703703703" y="1052" link="" x="757" uniqueID="element22" fileType="IMAGEFILTER" templateID="" type="ImportedImage" zoom="1" offsetY="0" offsetX="0" onPublish="ImportedImage" onPreview="ImportedImage" bucketRatio="0.6828703703703703" imageRatio="0.6828703703703703" rotate="0" brightness="0" contrast="0" colorEffect="" tintRed="0" tintGreen="0" tintBlue="0" borderWidth="0" borderColor="#52A8EC" borderRadius="0" boxShadow="none" glowColor="#52A8EC" origWidth="187" origHeight="274"/><?property ignore-whitespace="false"?><Element sizePositionLocked="false" border-color="#000000" type="Text" uniqueID="element23" deletable="" border-width="0" y="1308" x="761" width="176" editable="true" name="Text-15" height="20" padding="0" groupID="7" bgcolor="" locked="" templateID=""><Paragraph><Segment font-style="bold">Tamba, Kamakura, 1200 ad </Segment></Paragraph></Element><?property ignore-whitespace="true"?><?property ignore-whitespace="false"?><Element border-color="#000000" editable="true" border-width="0" y="1377" name="Text-16" x="45" templateID="" width="903" locked="" bgcolor="" type="Text" padding="0" deletable="" sizePositionLocked="false" height="111" uniqueID="element24"><Paragraph><Segment font-size="12" font-style="bold" color="#F99976">Tea bowls are synonymous with Japanese pottery. These humble forms were originally made in great quantities, providing functional ware for Japan's great pastime-drinking tea . Each piece, though mass produced was a symbol of a particular potter's body of work. In some Japanese workshops, an apprentice might make 500 tea bowls, pick one, and then destroy all the others. That one tea bowl would be the essence of that potter's work, and the apprentice would discover his style.</Segment></Paragraph><Paragraph><Segment>Click here to add text.</Segment></Paragraph></Element><?property ignore-whitespace="true"?><Element isImageType="true" sizePositionLocked="false" name="Add an Image-9" link_target="_self" maintainProportion="true" originalSource="" src="Seto__12-13th_c.jpg" height="209" alt="" editable="true" resizeable="true" locked="" deletable="" groupID="8" width="306" WOHratio="1.464406779661017" y="1506" link="" x="138" uniqueID="element25" fileType="IMAGEFILTER" templateID="" type="ImportedImage" zoom="1" offsetY="0" offsetX="0" onPublish="ImportedImage" onPreview="ImportedImage" bucketRatio="1.464406779661017" imageRatio="1.464406779661017" rotate="0" brightness="0" contrast="0" colorEffect="" tintRed="0" tintGreen="0" tintBlue="0" borderWidth="0" borderColor="#52A8EC" borderRadius="0" boxShadow="none" glowColor="#52A8EC" origWidth="254" origHeight="174"/><?property ignore-whitespace="false"?><Element sizePositionLocked="false" border-color="#000000" type="Text" uniqueID="element26" deletable="" border-width="0" y="1694" x="150" width="293" editable="true" name="Text-17" height="16" padding="0" groupID="8" bgcolor="" locked="" templateID=""><Paragraph><Segment font-style="bold">Seto                                             1200-1300 ad</Segment></Paragraph></Element><?property ignore-whitespace="true"?><Element isImageType="true" sizePositionLocked="false" name="Add an Image-10" link_target="_self" maintainProportion="true" originalSource="" src="Karatsu_16-17th_c.jpg" height="212" alt="" editable="true" resizeable="true" locked="" deletable="" groupID="9" width="261" WOHratio="1.2272727272727273" y="1496" link="" x="551" uniqueID="element27" fileType="IMAGEFILTER" templateID="" type="ImportedImage" zoom="1" offsetY="0" offsetX="0" onPublish="ImportedImage" onPreview="ImportedImage" bucketRatio="1.2272727272727273" imageRatio="1.2272727272727273" rotate="0" brightness="0" contrast="0" colorEffect="" tintRed="0" tintGreen="0" tintBlue="0" borderWidth="0" borderColor="#52A8EC" borderRadius="0" boxShadow="none" glowColor="#52A8EC" origWidth="261" origHeight="212"/><?property ignore-whitespace="false"?><Element sizePositionLocked="false" border-color="#000000" type="Text" uniqueID="element28" deletable="" border-width="0" y="1692" x="553" width="286" editable="true" name="Text-18" height="20" padding="0" groupID="9" bgcolor="" locked="" templateID=""><Paragraph><Segment font-style="bold">Karatsu                                1600-1700 ad</Segment></Paragraph></Element><?property ignore-whitespace="true"?><?property ignore-whitespace="false"?><Element border-color="#000000" editable="true" border-width="0" y="1758" name="Text-19" x="55" templateID="" width="899" locked="" bgcolor="" type="Text" padding="0" deletable="" sizePositionLocked="false" height="114" uniqueID="element29"><Paragraph><Segment font-size="12" font-style="bold" color="#F99976">As you look upon the tea bowls in this presentation, think how your hands would lovingly fondle each piece, and how your eyes might linger on the inside of each bowl, gazing at the pattern made by tea leaves. This is the core of Japanese folk pottery. The concept of the “Unknown Craftsman” originated from the every day humble ware produced by all the unknown potters working in the unconscious spirit of the collective soul. This philosophy was passed down by generations of potters resulting in a spontaneous interaction with clay and a total command of the materials. Having no need for identity, much of this ware remained unsigned.</Segment><Segment>Click here to add text.</Segment></Paragraph></Element><?property ignore-whitespace="true"?><Element isImageType="true" sizePositionLocked="false" name="Add an Image-11" link_target="_self" maintainProportion="true" originalSource="" src="Iga_c_1585-1700_.jpg" height="263" alt="" editable="true" resizeable="true" locked="" deletable="" groupID="29" width="159" WOHratio="0.6064814814814815" y="1918" link="" x="121" uniqueID="element30" fileType="IMAGEFILTER" templateID="" type="ImportedImage" zoom="1" offsetY="0" offsetX="0" onPublish="ImportedImage" onPreview="ImportedImage" bucketRatio="0.6064814814814815" imageRatio="0.6064814814814815" rotate="0" brightness="0" contrast="0" colorEffect="" tintRed="0" tintGreen="0" tintBlue="0" borderWidth="0" borderColor="#52A8EC" borderRadius="0" boxShadow="none" glowColor="#52A8EC" origWidth="164" origHeight="271"/><?property ignore-whitespace="false"?><Element sizePositionLocked="false" border-color="#000000" type="Text" uniqueID="element31" deletable="" border-width="0" y="2100" x="124" width="51" editable="true" name="Text-20" height="80" padding="0" groupID="29" bgcolor="" locked="" templateID=""><Paragraph><Segment font-style="bold"> Iga       </Segment></Paragraph><Paragraph><Segment font-style="bold">   </Segment></Paragraph><Paragraph><Segment font-style="bold">         1585-1700 ad</Segment></Paragraph></Element><?property ignore-whitespace="true"?><Element isImageType="true" sizePositionLocked="false" name="Add an Image-12" link_target="_self" maintainProportion="true" originalSource="" src="Oribe_16th_c.jpg" height="202" alt="" editable="true" resizeable="true" locked="" deletable="" groupID="11" width="284" WOHratio="1.4071661237785016" y="1938" link="" x="340" uniqueID="element32" fileType="IMAGEFILTER" templateID="" type="ImportedImage" zoom="1" offsetY="0" offsetX="0" onPublish="ImportedImage" onPreview="ImportedImage" bucketRatio="1.4071661237785016" imageRatio="1.4071661237785016" rotate="0" brightness="0" contrast="0" colorEffect="" tintRed="0" tintGreen="0" tintBlue="0" borderWidth="0" borderColor="#52A8EC" borderRadius="0" boxShadow="none" glowColor="#52A8EC" origWidth="284" origHeight="202"/><?property ignore-whitespace="false"?><Element sizePositionLocked="false" border-color="#000000" type="Text" uniqueID="element33" deletable="" border-width="0" y="2123" x="345" width="300" editable="true" name="Text-21" height="20" padding="0" groupID="11" bgcolor="" locked="" templateID=""><Paragraph><Segment font-style="bold">Oribe Food Box                             16th C ad</Segment></Paragraph></Element><?property ignore-whitespace="true"?><Element isImageType="true" sizePositionLocked="false" name="Add an Image-13" link_target="_self" maintainProportion="true" originalSource="" src="Shino_16th_c.jpg" height="243" alt="" editable="true" resizeable="true" locked="" deletable="" groupID="12" width="261" WOHratio="1.0719602977667493" y="1917" link="" x="665" uniqueID="element34" fileType="IMAGEFILTER" templateID="" type="ImportedImage" zoom="1" offsetY="0" offsetX="0" onPublish="ImportedImage" onPreview="ImportedImage" bucketRatio="1.0719602977667493" imageRatio="1.0719602977667493" rotate="0" brightness="0" contrast="0" colorEffect="" tintRed="0" tintGreen="0" tintBlue="0" borderWidth="0" borderColor="#52A8EC" borderRadius="0" boxShadow="none" glowColor="#52A8EC" origWidth="261" origHeight="243"/><?property ignore-whitespace="false"?><Element sizePositionLocked="false" border-color="#000000" type="Text" uniqueID="element35" deletable="" border-width="0" y="2141" x="666" width="279" editable="true" name="Text-22" height="20" padding="0" groupID="12" bgcolor="" locked="" templateID=""><Paragraph><Segment font-style="bold">Shino                                         16th C ad</Segment></Paragraph></Element><?property ignore-whitespace="true"?><?property ignore-whitespace="false"?><Element border-color="#000000" editable="true" border-width="0" y="2241" name="Text-23" x="63" templateID="" width="883" locked="" bgcolor="" type="Text" padding="0" deletable="" sizePositionLocked="false" height="111" uniqueID="element36"><Paragraph><Segment font-size="12" font-style="bold" color="#F99976">Iga, Oribe and Shino ware maintained the traditional characteristics of Japanese folk pottery, with their uneven rims and thick, spontaneously flowing glazes.  As in the example of Oribe, Oribe was a family name, not an actual period. However, pottery evolved as the livelihood of not just one family, but its many generations that were to follow. We begin to see pottery designated by  family names, such as Oribe and Kenzan, and the practice of the Unknown Craftsmen diminished.</Segment></Paragraph><Paragraph><Segment>Click here to add text.</Segment></Paragraph></Element><?property ignore-whitespace="true"?><Element isImageType="true" sizePositionLocked="false" name="Add an Image-14" link_target="_self" maintainProportion="true" originalSource="" src="Kenzan_1664-1743.jpg" height="265" alt="" editable="true" resizeable="true" locked="" deletable="" groupID="13" width="181" WOHratio="0.6828703703703703" y="2386" link="" x="209" uniqueID="element37" fileType="IMAGEFILTER" templateID="" type="ImportedImage" zoom="1" offsetY="0" offsetX="0" onPublish="ImportedImage" onPreview="ImportedImage" bucketRatio="0.6828703703703703" imageRatio="0.6828703703703703" rotate="0" brightness="0" contrast="0" colorEffect="" tintRed="0" tintGreen="0" tintBlue="0" borderWidth="0" borderColor="#52A8EC" borderRadius="0" boxShadow="none" glowColor="#52A8EC" origWidth="181" origHeight="265"/><?property ignore-whitespace="false"?><Element sizePositionLocked="false" border-color="#000000" type="Text" uniqueID="element38" deletable="" border-width="0" y="2634" x="212" width="188" editable="true" name="Text-24" height="20" padding="0" groupID="13" bgcolor="" locked="" templateID=""><Paragraph><Segment font-style="bold">Kenzan                 1664-1743</Segment></Paragraph></Element><?property ignore-whitespace="true"?><Element isImageType="true" sizePositionLocked="false" name="Add an Image-15" link_target="_self" maintainProportion="true" originalSource="" src="Oribe_incense_burner_16th_c.jpg" height="254" alt="" editable="true" resizeable="true" locked="" deletable="" groupID="14" width="316" WOHratio="1.2485549132947977" y="2390" link="" x="518" uniqueID="element39" fileType="IMAGEFILTER" templateID="" type="ImportedImage" zoom="1" offsetY="0" offsetX="0" onPublish="ImportedImage" onPreview="ImportedImage" bucketRatio="1.2485549132947977" imageRatio="1.2485549132947977" rotate="0" brightness="0" contrast="0" colorEffect="" tintRed="0" tintGreen="0" tintBlue="0" borderWidth="0" borderColor="#52A8EC" borderRadius="0" boxShadow="none" glowColor="#52A8EC" origWidth="316" origHeight="254"/><?property ignore-whitespace="false"?><Element sizePositionLocked="false" border-color="#000000" type="Text" uniqueID="element40" deletable="" border-width="0" y="2628" x="526" width="300" editable="true" name="Text-25" height="20" padding="0" groupID="14" bgcolor="" locked="" templateID=""><Paragraph><Segment font-style="bold">Oribe Incense Burner 16th C ad</Segment></Paragraph></Element><?property ignore-whitespace="true"?><?property ignore-whitespace="false"?><Element border-color="#000000" editable="true" border-width="0" y="2715" name="Text-26" x="72" templateID="" width="872" locked="" bgcolor="" type="Text" padding="0" deletable="" sizePositionLocked="false" height="130" uniqueID="element41"><Paragraph><Segment font-size="12" font-style="bold" color="#F99976">As pottery evolved into more decorative containers, the paintings on the ware became more structured, and the Japanese claimed a vigorous retail market. During the 17th century, the demand for hand painted porcelain grew worldwide and the highly refined Nabeshima ware was produced. The Dutch East India Company shipped this Japanese Export Porcelain throughout the world. The work was so mass-produced; it lost much of the individualization of traditional folk pottery. However, the clean lines of this industrial ware provided the background for complex brushwork, making it once again, masterful work and outstanding in the competitive market.</Segment><Segment>Click here to add text.</Segment></Paragraph></Element><?property ignore-whitespace="true"?><Element isImageType="true" sizePositionLocked="false" name="Add an Image-16" link_target="_self" maintainProportion="true" originalSource="" src="Nabeshima_plate_1688-1736.jpg" height="208" alt="" editable="true" resizeable="true" locked="" deletable="" groupID="15" width="204" WOHratio="0.9837962962962963" y="2911" link="" x="103" uniqueID="element42" fileType="IMAGEFILTER" templateID="" type="ImportedImage" zoom="1" offsetY="0" offsetX="0" onPublish="ImportedImage" onPreview="ImportedImage" bucketRatio="0.9837962962962963" imageRatio="0.9837962962962963" rotate="0" brightness="0" contrast="0" colorEffect="" tintRed="0" tintGreen="0" tintBlue="0" borderWidth="0" borderColor="#52A8EC" borderRadius="0" boxShadow="none" glowColor="#52A8EC" origWidth="184" origHeight="188"/><?property ignore-whitespace="false"?><Element sizePositionLocked="false" border-color="#000000" type="Text" uniqueID="element43" deletable="" border-width="0" y="3097" x="105" width="149" editable="true" name="Text-27" height="20" padding="0" groupID="15" bgcolor="" locked="" templateID=""><Paragraph><Segment font-style="bold">Nabeshima 1688-1736</Segment></Paragraph></Element><?property ignore-whitespace="true"?><Element isImageType="true" sizePositionLocked="false" name="Add an Image-17" link_target="_self" maintainProportion="true" originalSource="" src="Nabashima_1628-1736.jpg" height="283" alt="" editable="true" resizeable="true" locked="" deletable="" groupID="18" width="165" WOHratio="0.5810185185185185" y="2870" link="" x="417" uniqueID="element44" fileType="IMAGEFILTER" templateID="" type="ImportedImage" zoom="1" offsetY="0" offsetX="0" onPublish="ImportedImage" onPreview="ImportedImage" bucketRatio="0.5810185185185185" imageRatio="0.5810185185185185" rotate="0" brightness="0" contrast="0" colorEffect="" tintRed="0" tintGreen="0" tintBlue="0" borderWidth="0" borderColor="#52A8EC" borderRadius="0" boxShadow="none" glowColor="#52A8EC" origWidth="144" origHeight="248"/><?property ignore-whitespace="false"?><Element sizePositionLocked="false" border-color="#000000" type="Text" uniqueID="element45" deletable="" border-width="0" y="3119" x="456" width="114" editable="true" name="Text-28" height="20" padding="0" groupID="18" bgcolor="" locked="" templateID=""><Paragraph><Segment font-style="bold">Nabeshima 16thC</Segment></Paragraph></Element><?property ignore-whitespace="true"?><Element isImageType="true" sizePositionLocked="false" name="Add an Image-18" link_target="_self" maintainProportion="true" originalSource="" src="Nabeshima_bowl_18th_c_.jpg" height="196" alt="" editable="true" resizeable="true" locked="" deletable="" groupID="17" width="269" WOHratio="1.3670886075949367" y="2908" link="" x="637" uniqueID="element46" fileType="IMAGEFILTER" templateID="" type="ImportedImage" zoom="1" offsetY="0" offsetX="0" onPublish="ImportedImage" onPreview="ImportedImage" bucketRatio="1.3670886075949367" imageRatio="1.3670886075949367" rotate="0" brightness="0" contrast="0" colorEffect="" tintRed="0" tintGreen="0" tintBlue="0" borderWidth="0" borderColor="#52A8EC" borderRadius="0" boxShadow="none" glowColor="#52A8EC" origWidth="244" origHeight="179"/><?property ignore-whitespace="false"?><Element sizePositionLocked="false" border-color="#000000" type="Text" uniqueID="element47" deletable="" border-width="0" y="3085" x="642" width="160" editable="true" name="Text-29" height="20" padding="0" groupID="17" bgcolor="" locked="" templateID=""><Paragraph><Segment font-style="bold">Nabeshima 18th C ad</Segment></Paragraph></Element><?property ignore-whitespace="true"?><?property ignore-whitespace="false"?><Element border-color="#000000" editable="true" border-width="0" y="3193" name="Text-30" x="88" templateID="" width="823" locked="" bgcolor="" type="Text" padding="0" deletable="" sizePositionLocked="false" height="149" uniqueID="element48"><Paragraph><Segment font-size="12" font-style="bold" color="#F99976">By the 20th century, the word “mingei” surfaced, which means “folk art”, or art made by the unconscious collective. This phrase was termed by the Japanese art critic Soetsu Yanagi (1890-1961) and the potters Shoji Hamada (1894-1978) and Kanjiro Kawai (1890-1966). They inspired a movement that rejuvenated the return to traditional folk art in Japan. These three men also founded the Japan Folk Art Association and published the journal, Mingei, which since 1931, is still in print. Then, Yanagi, Hamada and the English potter, Bernard Leach toured Europe and the United States in the 1950’s, lecturing to eager Western potters the aesthetics of hand made pottery and the Japanese concepts of beauty.</Segment></Paragraph><Paragraph><Segment>Click here to add text.</Segment></Paragraph></Element><?property ignore-whitespace="true"?><Element isImageType="true" sizePositionLocked="false" name="Add an Image-19" link_target="_self" maintainProportion="true" originalSource="" src="Hamada_20th_c.jpg" height="330" alt="" editable="true" resizeable="true" locked="" deletable="" groupID="32" width="230" WOHratio="0.6990740740740741" y="3380" link="" x="181" uniqueID="element49" fileType="IMAGEFILTER" templateID="" type="ImportedImage" zoom="1" offsetY="0" offsetX="0" onPublish="ImportedImage" onPreview="ImportedImage" bucketRatio="0.6990740740740741" imageRatio="0.6990740740740741" rotate="0" brightness="0" contrast="0" colorEffect="" tintRed="0" tintGreen="0" tintBlue="0" borderWidth="0" borderColor="#52A8EC" borderRadius="0" boxShadow="none" glowColor="#52A8EC" origWidth="231" origHeight="331"/><?property ignore-whitespace="false"?><Element sizePositionLocked="false" border-color="#000000" type="Text" uniqueID="element50" deletable="" border-width="0" y="3692" x="186" wi