false" name="Add an Image-23" link_target="_self" maintainProportion="true" originalSource="" src="Tsujimura__Shiro_b._1948.jpg" height="282" alt="" editable="true" resizeable="true" locked="" deletable="" groupID="23" width="232" WOHratio="0.8240740740740741" y="3686" link="" x="711" uniqueID="element57" fileType="IMAGEFILTER" templateID="" type="ImportedImage" zoom="1" offsetY="0" offsetX="0" onPublish="ImportedImage" onPreview="ImportedImage" bucketRatio="0.8240740740740741" imageRatio="0.8240740740740741" rotate="0" brightness="0" contrast="0" colorEffect="" tintRed="0" tintGreen="0" tintBlue="0" borderWidth="0" borderColor="#52A8EC" borderRadius="0" boxShadow="none" glowColor="#52A8EC" origWidth="232" origHeight="282"/><?property ignore-whitespace="false"?><Element sizePositionLocked="false" border-color="#000000" type="Text" uniqueID="element58" deletable="" border-width="0" y="3951" x="715" width="163" editable="true" name="Text-35" height="20" padding="0" groupID="23" bgcolor="" locked="" templateID=""><Paragraph><Segment font-style="bold">Tsujimura, Shiro, b. 1948</Segment></Paragraph></Element><?property ignore-whitespace="true"?><?property ignore-whitespace="false"?><Element border-color="#000000" editable="true" border-width="0" y="4137" name="Text-36" x="70" templateID="" width="300" locked="" bgcolor="" type="Text" padding="0" deletable="" sizePositionLocked="false" height="20" uniqueID="element59"><Paragraph><Segment>Click here to add text.</Segment></Paragraph></Element><?property ignore-whitespace="true"?><?property ignore-whitespace="false"?><Element border-color="#000000" editable="true" border-width="0" y="4114" name="Text-37" x="82" templateID="" width="823" locked="" bgcolor="" type="Text" padding="0" deletable="" sizePositionLocked="false" height="111" uniqueID="element60"><Paragraph><Segment font-size="12" font-style="bold" color="#F99976">They spoke of the irregular and the asymmetrical, and how these attributes characterize the traditional Japanese concept of beauty. Japanese ceramics was not just an influence in style, but also an exploration in philosophy. It was an inquiry into the speculative rather than the observational, and it created the freedom to design. American potters were thirsty for this unconstrained approach to clay and welcomed the new aesthetic. </Segment></Paragraph><Paragraph><Segment>Click here to add text.</Segment></Paragraph></Element><?property ignore-whitespace="true"?><?property ignore-whitespace="false"?><Element border-color="#000000" editable="true" border-width="0" y="4282" name="Text-38" x="82" templateID="" width="812" locked="" bgcolor="" type="Text" padding="0" deletable="" sizePositionLocked="false" height="114" uniqueID="element61"><Paragraph><Segment font-size="12" font-style="bold" color="#F99976">The latter part of the 20th century witnessed not only the return to handcrafted folk work but also the emergence of the "artist potter”. Individual Japanese studio potters produced elaborate one of a kind pieces, again proving the mastery of fine craftsmanship in the long tradition of Japanese ceramics. The beautiful vessels of Tommimoto and Tokuriki clearly reflect this mastery of detail, while the unconscious pottery of Hamada, Shimaoka and Tsujimura reveal the fabric of traditional folk pottery in a contemporary society. </Segment><Segment>Click here to add text.</Segment></Paragraph></Element><?property ignore-whitespace="true"?><Element isImageType="true" sizePositionLocked="false" name="Add an Image-24" link_target="_self" maintainProportion="true" originalSource="" src="Tomimoto_jar_20th_c.jpg" height="219" alt="" editable="true" resizeable="true" locked="" deletable="" groupID="" width="300" WOHratio="1.375796178343949" y="4466" link="" x="65" uniqueID="element62" fileType="IMAGEFILTER" templateID="" type="ImportedImage" zoom="1" offsetY="0" offsetX="0" onPublish="ImportedImage" onPreview="ImportedImage" bucketRatio="1.375796178343949" imageRatio="1.3757961783